From 59c2bd866af6cd4d71255e52bfdbb08a4a3ca946 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Keith Packard <keithp@keithp.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 08:02:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/22] stdio: Give streams no orientation until a byte or wide
 function is used

picolibc had only the wide flag, so a stream was byte-oriented from
the start: fwide(f, 0) returned -1 on a new stream, and fwide(f, 1)
made a stream wide even after byte I/O (fwide(f, -1) did the reverse).
POSIX (XSH 2.5.2): a stream starts without orientation, the first byte
or wide-character I/O function sets it, and only freopen and fwide on
an unoriented stream change it; fwide returns 0 for no orientation.

The new __SBYTE flag records byte orientation. getc, putc, fread,
fwrite, fputs, puts, ungetc and the byte printf and scanf functions set
it; the wide functions set __SWIDE; fwide sets either only on an
unoriented stream; freopen clears both. perror leaves an unoriented
stderr unoriented, as POSIX asks.
---
